.TH NNANO 1 "2026-11-09" "nnano 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nnano \- a modern, nano\-compatible terminal text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nnano
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nnano
is a terminal text editor that keeps nano's ethos \- simple, fast, easy,
comfortable \- with a desktop\-style keymap, mouse support, multiple buffers,
grouped undo and syntax highlighting from nano's
.B .nanorc
grammars.
.PP
With no
.I file
it opens an empty buffer. A
.I file
of
.B \-
reads standard input into a buffer with no name; saving it asks for a path.
.PP
Every option can also be set in
.I config.toml
and in the settings overlay
.RB ( Alt+, ).
The command line wins over the files, which win over
.BR ~/.nanorc ,
which wins over the built\-in defaults.
.PP
The right edge of the text area carries a one\-column scrollbar, and the status
bar shows
.IR line:column/total :
the cursor line, its visual column and the number of lines in the document.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-tabsize " " \fIN\fR
Columns a tab advances, 1\-64 (default 8). The same value drives the renderer
and the cursor arithmetic, so a tab never shifts the cursor off its glyph.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-casesensitive ", " \-\-no\-casesensitive
Make searches match case by default (default off). The find prompt can flip
this per search with
.BR Alt+C .
.TP
.BR \-\-syntax ", " \-\-no\-syntax
Colour lines with the grammar of the file's extension (default on).
.BR Alt+Y
toggles it for the session.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mouse ", " \-\-no\-mouse
Use the mouse: click places the cursor, drag marks, Alt+drag marks a rectangle,
the wheel scrolls without moving the cursor, and clicking the scrollbar on the
right jumps to that part of the file (default on).
.TP
.BR \-\-osc52 ", " \-\-no\-osc52
Also send a copy to the system clipboard over OSC 52, capped at 64 KiB
(default off). The internal register always holds the text; the cap is reported
in the status bar when it truncates.
.TP
.BI \-\-language " LANG"
Interface language:
.BR auto ,
.BR en ,
.BR zh-Hans ,
.BR hi ,
.BR es ,
.BR ar ,
.BR fr ,
.BR bn ,
.BR pt ,
.BR id
or
.BR ja
(default
.BR en ).
.B auto
follows
.BR $LC_ALL ,
.B $LC_MESSAGES
or
.BR $LANG .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
The full reference is the
.B Ctrl+H
overlay inside the editor; it is generated from the same table the decoder
reads, so it lists every implemented binding.
.nf

  Files      Ctrl+S save in place            Alt+S save as
             Ctrl+O open by path             Ctrl+R reload from disk
             Alt+I  insert a file            Ctrl+W close buffer
             Ctrl+Q quit                     Ctrl+N new buffer
  Editing    Ctrl+Z undo   Ctrl+Y redo       Ctrl+X cut    Ctrl+C copy
             Ctrl+V paste                     Ctrl+K cut line
             Ctrl+A select all               Alt+A start mark
             Alt+Z  rectangular mark         Ctrl+D delete character
             Ctrl+J justify                  Ctrl+\\ replace
             Alt+X  run a command            Alt+Y  toggle syntax
  Move       arrows, Home/End, PgUp/PgDn     Ctrl+left/right words
             Shift+arrows extend the mark: left/right by characters,
             up/down by lines, keeping the visual column
             Ctrl+G go to line               Alt+\\ first line  Alt+/ last
  Search     Ctrl+F find                     F3 / Shift+F3 next / previous
             Alt+C  case (in a prompt)       Alt+R  regexp (in a prompt)
  Interface  Ctrl+P palette                  Alt+, settings
             Ctrl+H help                     Ctrl+L refresh
.fi
.PP
A dirty buffer confirms before
.B Ctrl+Q
or
.BR Ctrl+W ,
and a dirty reload confirms before
.BR Ctrl+R .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nnano/config.toml
User configuration; falls back to
.IR ~/.config/nnano/config.toml .
Parsed as TOML and validated against the option registry: an unknown key warns
once and is ignored.
.TP
.I /etc/nnano/config.toml
Optional system configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.nanorc
Legacy nano configuration, read only. nano and nnano are safe to use side by
side; nnano never writes, rewrites or migrates it.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/nnano/recovery/
Recovery snapshots of dirty buffers, one per file, written 2 seconds after the
last edit and removed on save, on close and on a clean exit. Falls back to
.IR ~/.local/state/nnano/recovery/ .
.TP
.I ~/.config/nnano/syntax/*.nanorc
Extra syntax grammars, in nano's format. Distro grammars are read from
.IR /usr/share/nnano/syntax " and " /usr/share/nano ,
and a built\-in set covering the popular languages is always available, so nano
does not have to be installed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the user configuration lives when set.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the recovery snapshots live when set.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
Read only for
.BR "\-\-language auto" .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The editor closed normally.
.TP
.B 1
The terminal could not be initialised, or a file could not be read.
.SH NOTES
.B Ctrl+S
and
.B Ctrl+Q
need a terminal without XON/XOFF flow control and with
.B ISIG
off; nnano sets both up itself before the first frame.
.PP
Saving is in place: the file is truncated and rewritten, then flushed and
fsynced. There is no
.I file~
backup and no temp\-file rename, so hardlinks, symlinks and watched inodes
survive \- and an interrupted write leaves a truncated file. The recovery
snapshot is the only net.
.PP
Encoding is detected, never transcoded: UTF\-8 when the bytes parse as UTF\-8,
Latin\-1 otherwise, and UTF\-16 is refused instead of guessed. Arabic is drawn in
logical order and the terminal decides the visual order; there is no bidi
reshaping.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR nano (1),
.BR vi (1)
.PP
Full documentation:
.IR https://github.com/nestorPons/neo\-nano .
.SH BUGS
Report at
.IR https://github.com/nestorPons/neo\-nano/issues .
